      <p>In practice, most care providers use LMP (last menstrual period) for initial dating and then confirm or adjust with a first-trimester ultrasound. If the two methods differ by more than 7 days, the ultrasound measurement typically takes precedence.</p>
      <p>When I've worked through due date questions with people, the most common mistake is using an LMP date from memory rather than checking a calendar or app. Even being off by one week on the LMP date moves the due date by a full week - which can affect decisions about induction timing later in pregnancy.</p>
      <h2 id="gestational-age">What is gestational age exactly?</h2>
      <p><a href="/glossary/gestational-age">Gestational age</a> is the age of a pregnancy measured in weeks and days from the first day of the last menstrual period. It's the universal language of <a href="/glossary/obstetrics">obstetrics</a> - every screening test, viability threshold, and developmental milestone is expressed in gestational weeks.</p>
      <p>A pregnancy that reaches 40 weeks gestational age is considered full term, though 37-41 weeks is the normal delivery range (WHO, 2015).</p>
      <p>Gestational age differs from <a href="/glossary/fetal-age">fetal age</a> (also called fertilization age or embryonic age) by approximately two weeks. Fetal age counts from conception; gestational age counts from LMP.</p>
      <p>When a scan report says your baby is "22 weeks 3 days," that's gestational age. When a fetal development book says "at 20 weeks the baby can hear," that may be measuring fetal age - so be aware of which measure a source is using.</p>
