      <h2 id="fetal-development">How big is your baby? Fetal size by trimester</h2>
      <p>Fetal size comparisons to fruit are a widely used way to visualize growth - and they're clinically grounded in crown-rump length (CRL) measurements taken by ultrasound.</p>
      <p>At week 8, the embryo is about 16 mm long, roughly the size of a raspberry. By week 20 it's a 161-mm banana. At term (week 40), the average newborn is about 50 cm and 3.5 kg (<a href="/conversion/length-converter/cm-to-in">19.7 in</a> and <a href="/conversion/weight-converter/kg-to-lb">7.7 lbs</a>) - the size of a watermelon.</p>
      <p>The size displayed in the calculator is based on median CRL and biparietal diameter data from standard <a href="/glossary/obstetrics">obstetric growth charts</a>.</p>
      <p>Individual babies vary significantly - a baby measuring slightly smaller or larger than the median is completely normal. Your sonographer will flag any measurements outside the normal range.</p>
