      <h2 id="accuracy">How accurate is your due date?</h2>
      <p>Due dates are estimates, not predictions. Only about 5% of babies arrive on the exact EDD (Mongelli et al., 1996). Most full-term deliveries happen between weeks 37 and 41.</p>
      <p>Accuracy depends heavily on the method used: first-trimester ultrasound is the most reliable, while LMP-based dating assumes ovulation on exactly day 14 of a perfect 28-day cycle - which doesn't describe most people's cycles.</p>
      <p>ACOG recommends adjusting the LMP-based EDD if ultrasound dating differs by more than 7 days in the first trimester, 10 days in the second, or 14 days in the third. So if your LMP puts you at 10 weeks but your ultrasound shows 11 weeks 2 days, your provider will update your due date to match the scan.</p>
      <p>After 20 weeks, ultrasound accuracy drops significantly - a third-trimester scan can be off by up to 3 weeks.</p>
      <p>One thing I've seen often: people use the calculator to track their due date, see it's a Thursday, and mentally commit to that date. The healthier framing is to think of a 4-week window - "sometime between [date - 2 weeks] and [date + 2 weeks]" - and plan accordingly.</p>
      <p>Having baby gear, support, and leave arranged by week 37 is far more useful than fixating on a single day.</p>
      <h2 id="faq">FAQs about due date and gestational age</h2>
      <h3>How is the due date calculated from the last period?</h3>
      <p>Using Naegele's rule, the estimated due date is the first day of your last menstrual period plus 280 days (40 weeks). This formula assumes a 28-day cycle. For longer or shorter cycles, the calculator adjusts by the difference from 28 days - so a 30-day cycle pushes the EDD 2 days later, and a 25-day cycle moves it 3 days earlier.</p>
      <h3>What is gestational age, and how is it different from fetal age?</h3>
      <p>Gestational age counts from the first day of your last period; fetal age (embryonic age) counts from conception. They differ by about 2 weeks. All clinical milestones - viability at week 24, full term at week 37, post-term at week 42 - use gestational age. When a book or app says "your baby at week X," check whether it's using gestational age or fetal age to avoid confusion.</p>
      <h3>How accurate is an ultrasound due date compared with LMP dating?</h3>
      <p>A first-trimester ultrasound measuring crown-rump length is accurate to within 5-7 days and is considered the gold standard (Kalish et al., 2004). LMP dating can be off by up to 2 weeks, especially with irregular cycles or late ovulation. ACOG recommends adjusting the due date to match the ultrasound if the two methods differ by more than 7 days at under 14 weeks.</p>
      <h3>How does an IVF transfer date change the calculation?</h3>
      <p>For IVF pregnancies, gestational age on transfer day equals 2 weeks plus the embryo's age (14 + 3 = 17 days for Day 3; 14 + 5 = 19 days for Day 5; 14 + 6 = 20 days for Day 6). The equivalent LMP is set by subtracting that gestational age from the transfer date. IVF due dates are the most accurate of all methods because fertilization timing is precisely documented.</p>
      <h3>What does "full term" mean, and when is it safe to deliver?</h3>
      <p>Full term begins at 37 completed weeks of gestational age. Deliveries from 37 to 38+6 weeks are called "early term," 39-40+6 weeks are "full term," and 41-41+6 weeks are "late term." Post-term is 42 weeks or more, when induction is typically offered. The optimal delivery window for most uncomplicated pregnancies is 39-40 weeks, when neonatal outcomes are best (ACOG, 2013).</p>
